    pity_explanation_html: `<p>MultiVersus' battle pass features guaranteed linear cosmetic progression based on seasonal playtime. Zero randomness exists—all cosmetic tiers are predetermined and guaranteed through deterministic progression. Players unlock cosmetics through challenges and gameplay hours without any probability variance. This ensures complete transparency about earned rewards without gambling mechanics or loot-based randomness.</p><p>Cosmetic acquisition is purely deterministic based on effort rather than luck. Free players can earn cosmetics through free cosmetic tier progression and seasonal events, creating viable completely free cosmetic experience. All cosmetics earned are permanent account acquisitions never affected by rotation or temporary availability mechanics.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2022 (May):</strong> MultiVersus launches as Warner Bros.' free-to-play fighting game with cosmetic monetization and all fighters free model.</p><p><strong>2023 (Mid-Year):</strong> Game transitions to improved cosmetic model after player feedback on original monetization opacity and cosmetic pricing concerns.</p><p><strong>2023 (Late):</strong> Cosmetic transparency dramatically improves with clearer pricing displays and bundle previews. UI improvements reduce cosmetic confusion and purchasing regret.</p><p><strong>2024 (Early):</strong> MultiVersus continues cosmetic-only monetization with transparent pricing and regular cosmetic rotations. Community sentiment improves with monetization clarity.</p><p><strong>2024 (Current):</strong> Game maintains fair fighting game monetization with all fighters permanently free and cosmetics clearly priced. Developer responsiveness to monetization feedback establishes player trust.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>MultiVersus' primary criticism involved unclear cosmetic pricing in early launches, creating player confusion about cosmetic value and rotating availability. This has substantially improved through transparency updates and cosmetic communication. Some cosmetics are exclusive to seasonal battle passes, creating perception of limited availability. However, cosmetics rotate through seasonal stores and price transparency has increased dramatically, addressing historical concerns about predatory pricing.</p><p>Secondary concerns include cosmetic bundle bundling forcing package purchases rather than individual cosmetic sales, and occasional cosmetic pricing escalation. However, these criticisms remain mild compared to competitors—cosmetics provide zero competitive advantage, and ethical monetization is increasingly evident through developer communication and transparency improvements.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1 (All Fighters Free):</strong> All fighters are permanently free—no grinding or paywalls required whatsoever. Focus your engagement on gameplay mastery and mechanical improvement instead of fighter acquisition gating.</p><p><strong>Tip 2 (Battle Pass Cosmetics):</strong> Battle pass cosmetics are substantial in quantity and quality. Complete free cosmetic tier progression extensively before purchasing any cosmetics with real money.</p><p><strong>Tip 3 (Cosmetic Rotation Strategy):</strong> Cosmetic bundles rotate seasonally and through seasonal stores. Wait for cosmetic rotations instead of purchasing at full Gleamium price to maximize value.</p><p><strong>Tip 4 (Fighting Game Cosmetics):</strong> Character cosmetics affect visibility minimally in fighting games (purely first-person or cosmetic perspective). Prioritize cosmetics you enjoy aesthetically over hypothetical gameplay perception concerns.</p><p><strong>Tip 5 (Retroactive Cosmetic Discounts):</strong> Track seasonal cosmetic pricing carefully. Warner Bros. occasionally discounts cosmetics retroactively after seasonal launch, rewarding patient buyers who wait.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>MultiVersus represents an improving cosmetic monetization model that demonstrated developer responsiveness to player feedback. Early iterations had significant transparency issues and cosmetic pricing confusion, but the game has evolved toward clearer pricing structures and fairer cosmetic distribution. The $9.99 seasonal pass is fairly priced for 100+ cosmetic rewards, and all fighters being permanently free eliminates pay-to-win concerns entirely.</p><p>The game's competitive advantage: fighting game cosmetics have minimal gameplay perception impact compared to team shooters or MOBAs where cosmetics create visibility or psychological pressure. Cosmetics are genuinely optional without creating perception of competitive disadvantage. Gleamium pricing at $6-15 per cosmetic bundle is reasonable for the genre, though individual cosmetic pricing could improve further to match competitors like Helldivers 2.</p><p>Recommendation: MultiVersus has fair free-to-play monetization with notable improvement trajectory. All fighters permanently free, cosmetics clearly optional, zero pay-to-win mechanics in fights themselves. Primary advantage over competitors is fighting game mechanics—cosmetics matter far less competitively than team shooters. Recommended for fighting game enthusiasts who value fair monetization and genre mechanics over cosmetic philosophy.</p>`
