    history_html: `<p><strong>2012 (Original Beta):</strong> Original SMITE launches with cosmetic-focused monetization and free god rotation model preventing pay-to-win accusations from inception.</p><p><strong>2018-2023 (Years):</strong> SMITE establishes stable cosmetic monetization with transparent pricing and regular cosmetic releases. Hi-Rez proves cosmetic-only sustainability across decade-long support.</p><p><strong>2023 (Announcement):</strong> SMITE 2 announced with modernized engine and refined cosmetic systems improving transparency and monetization structure.</p><p><strong>2024 (Launch):</strong> SMITE 2 launches with refined cosmetic model, maintaining free gods and cosmetic-only spending philosophy from original while improving cosmetic transparency.</p><p><strong>2024 (Current):</strong> SMITE 2 continues cosmetic-only monetization proving Hi-Rez's long-term commitment to fair MOBA pricing and god accessibility.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>SMITE 2's primary concerns involve cosmetic exclusivity—some limited-time cosmetics may rotate without returning immediately, creating perception of artificial scarcity despite Hi-Rez's generally fair rotation practices. Additionally, cosmetic pricing escalation from original SMITE ($400+ Gems for premium cosmetics) creates perception of value reduction and price inflation. However, cosmetics provide zero competitive advantage and rotations eventually return cosmetics to availability.</p><p>Secondary concerns include cosmetic bundle pricing pressure and potential pay-to-cosmetic-complete culture among collectors. However, these criticisms remain mild compared to competitors like League of Legends ($20+ cosmetics) or Valorant ($15-30 cosmetics) with significantly higher pricing structures.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1 (All Gods Free):</strong> All gods are permanently free through rotation and progression without cosmetic gates. Focus your engagement on mechanical mastery rather than god acquisition pressure.</p><p><strong>Tip 2 (Battle Pass Value):</strong> $9.99 battle pass provides 100+ cosmetic rewards worth substantial value compared to individual cosmetic purchases. Complete free tiers extensively before purchasing any cosmetics.</p><p><strong>Tip 3 (Cosmetic Rotation Strategy):</strong> Cosmetics rotate seasonally and through cosmetic shops on predictable schedules. Wait for cosmetic rotations rather than purchasing at launch pricing for better value preservation.</p><p><strong>Tip 4 (Gem Efficiency):</strong> Track Gem-to-cosmetic value ratios carefully. Bundle purchases frequently offer 15-25% better value per cosmetic than individual cosmetic acquisitions.</p><p><strong>Tip 5 (Free Event Cosmetics):</strong> Participate in seasonal events and Twitch drops actively. SMITE 2 regularly distributes cosmetics without spending requirements through community engagement.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>SMITE 2 demonstrates Hi-Rez's successful translation of cosmetic-only monetization from original SMITE to modern sequel. The $9.99 seasonal pass is fairly priced for 100+ tiers, and cosmetics provide zero competitive advantage ensuring fair competitive environment. Gem pricing at $6-15 per cosmetic bundle is reasonable for the genre, though cosmetic inflation from original SMITE is notable and concerning for long-term player satisfaction.</p><p>The game's competitive advantage: free god rotation prevents pay-to-win accusations completely, distinguishing SMITE 2 from League of Legends where champions require grinding or payment. Unlike League where cosmetics create psychological spending pressure, SMITE 2 cosmetics feel genuinely optional due to rotational access and free progression. Hi-Rez's monetization transparency and cosmetic library preservation creates baseline player trust.</p><p>Recommendation: SMITE 2 has fair MOBA monetization with all gods permanently free and cosmetics clearly optional. Cosmetic pricing is moderate for MOBAs though higher than optimal. Recommended for MOBA players valuing fair god access over cosmetic-focused spending or those preferring mythological cosmetic themes over pop culture synergies.</p>`
