    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1 (One-Time Purchase):</strong> Buy the game once ($69.99) during sales if possible. All characters and core gameplay are permanently unlocked—zero additional grinding or character paywalls required.</p><p><strong>Tip 2 (Battle Pass Optional):</strong> Battle pass ($9.99) is entirely optional cosmetics only. Free cosmetics are available through gameplay challenges without spending.</p><p><strong>Tip 3 (Direct Cosmetic Purchases):</strong> Cosmetics are directly purchasable without randomness involved. Buy specific cosmetics you aesthetically prefer instead of gambling with loot systems.</p><p><strong>Tip 4 (Cosmetic Gameplay Impact):</strong> Fighting game cosmetics have minimal competitive impact. Prioritize cosmetics you enjoy aesthetically over hypothetical gameplay advantage perception.</p><p><strong>Tip 5 (Cosmetic Rotation Patience):</strong> Seasonal cosmetics rotate but Namco occasionally reruns popular cosmetics. Wait for cosmetic rotations instead of FOMO purchasing at launch pricing.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>Tekken 8's monetization is exemplary for premium games and establishes standard premium fighting games should match. The $69.99 upfront cost is investment-honest—you're purchasing a complete game, not entering service-based extraction design. Post-purchase monetization is purely cosmetic with zero FOMO exploitation or gameplay gating.</p><p>The game's cosmetic model is sophisticated and fair: cosmetics are directly purchasable (eliminating loot mechanics), battle pass is genuinely optional, and cosmetics provide zero gameplay advantage. Fighting game genre naturally amplifies this fairness—cosmetics don't affect competitive perception like they do in MOBAs or shooters where cosmetics create visibility concerns.</p><p>Recommendation: Tekken 8 is the standard premium fighting game monetization should achieve. The $69.99 initial cost is reasonable for complete game upfront purchase without service model extraction. Cosmetics are optional and fairly priced at $5-15. Recommended for players prioritizing fair monetization and fighting game depth over free-to-play accessibility. This game proves premium monetization model is ethical alternative to cosmetic-extraction live-service games.</p>`
